# remotix — the PAM service of the REMOTIX server.  ⭐ DEBIAN AND UBUNTU (and Mint).
#
#   installed in /etc/pam.d/remotix   (done by `costruisci.sh`)
#
# ⭐ PHASE 17 — one file per family, like Cockpit (`fasi/17-l-installatore.md`
#    §4.3): `@include` below is a Debian modification to Linux-PAM, and
#    elsewhere the line is "illegal module type".  The other three:
#      remotix.pam.fedora   Fedora, RHEL, Rocky, Alma   → /etc/pam.d/remotix
#      remotix.pam.suse     openSUSE                    → /usr/lib/pam.d/remotix
#      remotix.pam.arch     Arch, Manjaro               → /etc/pam.d/remotix
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⛔ WHY IT EXISTS, AND WHY `login` IS NOT USED
#
# `SPECIFICHE.md` §4.2, first line: "local PAM, service `remotix`, with the ban
# of the address after three failed attempts".  Until 10 August 2026 the code
# said `login` (finding B-11), and the price was not one of form: on Debian
# `/etc/pam.d/login` is the LOCAL CONSOLE stack.  Inside it there are
# `pam_securetty` (which reasons on /etc/securetty and on a terminal that is not
# here), `pam_lastlog`, `pam_motd`, `pam_limits`, `pam_selinux`, `pam_keyinit`
# — policies meant for whoever sits in front of the machine.  ⚠ A network
# access that passes there inherits them all, and which behaviour changes is not
# even predictable by reading: it depends on how that machine is configured.
#
# ⚠ And there is a consequence on the MEASUREMENTS: the median of the rejected attempts that
#   B8 measured on the night of 10 August — 2636 ms — is the median of `login`,
#   not of this service.  It must be redone, and the `[?]` on the fixed second of
#   §4.4-bis is not closed with that one.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⛔ IF THIS FILE IS NOT THERE, EVERY RIGHT PASSWORD IS REFUSED
#
# Linux-PAM, when the requested service has no file, falls back to the service
# `other`.  ⛔ What `other` does depends on the distribution: on Fedora and
# Arch it is `pam_deny` (the symptom would be "wrong user or
# password" for anyone, that is a diagnosis pointing at the password
# while the defect is a missing file); on Debian it includes the common stacks —
# and it is not better: it is a stack that is not ours (see §session below,
# 15 August 2026, and the exclusion of root that is not there).  Hence the two
# protections, which live in the program (invariant I7):
#
#   1. `main.c` looks at /etc/pam.d/remotix (and /usr/lib/pam.d/remotix, where
#      openSUSE puts it) at startup and SAYS so if it is not there;
#   2. `autenticazione.c` tells apart in the log "PAM refused" from "PAM could not
#      judge", and names this file in the second.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠ The common stacks are INCLUDED instead of rewritten: `CODER.md` §4.1,
#   "depend, do not rewrite".  Whoever administers the machine configures
#   `common-auth` once and it holds for all services, this one included.
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⭐⭐ D3 CLOSED (`DECISIONI.md` §10.18, 30 Sep 2026) — REMOTIX MIRRORS
#    the system's authentication: *"I do not want REMOTIX to drift
#    from the system's default authentication, it must mirror
#    PAM"*.  ⇒ From here down it is Debian's and Ubuntu's /etc/pam.d/sshd, line by
#    line, with ONE extra line at the top: root excluded (like stock ssh).
#    `[M]` 30 Sep, comparison with /etc/pam.d/sshd on debian13-gnome-iso and
#    ubuntu2604-gnome-iso (identical to each other): 15 lines of 15 equal
#    (`banchi/17-t6/t6-leggi-pam.sh`).  The lines that were there before (the
#    `common-session-noninteractive` stack + `pam_systemd` by name, `pam_nologin`
#    in auth) were a policy of OURS: the «session» § below tells
#    why they were born, and why the rule of D3 supersedes them — stock `common-session`
#    carries `pam_systemd` (`[M]` Debian 13 and Ubuntu 26.04), and what
#    is extra (`pam_motd`, `pam_mail`, `pam_limits`, `pam_env`) is the
#    same that whoever logs in with ssh receives.
#    ⛔ The old objection to `login` stays true: `login` is the
#       CONSOLE stack (pam_securetty & co.); sshd is the NETWORK access stack, and
#       REMOTIX is a network access.

# ── auth: "is this password right?"
#
# ⭐ ROOT EXCLUDED, and BEFORE the password (`fasi/17` §4.3).  It is the only
#    intended difference from sshd (D3).  The file lists one name per line; it is
#    installed with `root` in it, owned by root and not writable by others
#    (`pam_listfile` refuses it otherwise).  Cockpit does the same with
#    /etc/cockpit/disallowed-users.
#  · in `auth` and `requisite`, not in `account`: with the line in `account` the
#    root password would be TRIED anyway, and "right password but account
#    denied" answers differently from "wrong password" — an oracle for whoever
#    guesses root's password from the network;
#  · ⛔ `onerr=fail` (Cockpit uses `succeed`): if the file is missing or badly
#    written NOBODY gets in, and PAM's log says so.  With `succeed` the
#    lost file would silently remove root's exclusion — a
#    protection in a line that can be lost, that is what I7 forbids.
auth       requisite    pam_listfile.so item=user sense=deny file=/etc/remotix/utenti-negati onerr=fail
#
# ⭐ `pam_faillock`, if the administrator has put it in `common-auth`, comes
#   with the stack: it holds for REMOTIX as for ssh (D3).
@include common-auth

# ── account: "is this account usable?"  (account expired, locked, ...)
#    ⚠ It is not an extra: `pam_authenticate` does not answer this question, and
#      `autenticazione.c` calls `pam_acct_mgmt` on purpose.
account    required     pam_nologin.so
@include common-account

# ── session: `figlio.c` calls `pam_open_session`, and it is that call that makes
#    the user's logind session be born — with `pam_systemd`, which is in
#    `common-session` (`[M]` 30 Sep: Debian 13 and Ubuntu 26.04).
#
# ⛔ HISTORY, 15 August 2026 (phase 5): back then the file included
#    `common-session-noninteractive`, which does NOT have `pam_systemd` — and without it,
#    the user has no logind session, Mutter's `is_headless()` has
#    nothing to look at (`DECISIONI.md` §4.3-bis) and the rule of §5.1 loses its
#    subject.  The defect was invisible by accident: the file was not
#    installed and PAM fell back to `other`, which on Debian includes the common
#    stacks.  The cure back then was `pam_systemd` by name; with D3 the cure is
#    sshd's stack, which carries it.  ⛔ The proof that remains: `provisiona.sh`
#    checks that the installed file reaches `pam_systemd`.
#
# ⚠ `pam_loginuid` `required` like sshd: the service (and `riavvia-7700.sh`,
#   with `systemd-run`) starts from systemd, without loginuid, and can write it.
# ⚠ `pam_selinux` is there as in sshd, with the actions that silence it where the
#   module is not there (stock Debian and Ubuntu are without SELinux).
session [success=ok ignore=ignore module_unknown=ignore default=bad]        pam_selinux.so close
session    required     pam_loginuid.so
session    optional     pam_keyinit.so force revoke
@include common-session
session    optional     pam_motd.so  motd=/run/motd.dynamic
session    optional     pam_motd.so noupdate
session    optional     pam_mail.so standard noenv
session    required     pam_limits.so
session    required     pam_env.so
session    required     pam_env.so envfile=/etc/default/locale
session [success=ok ignore=ignore module_unknown=ignore default=bad]        pam_selinux.so open
@include common-password
